  "answer_text": "### Experiment Report\n\n**6.1 Experiment Objective**\nThe objective was to acquire Raman spectra over a 5x5 grid with 0.1 mm spacing, covering an area from +0.0 to +0.4 mm in both X and Y directions relative to the initial stage position (37.8759, 25.248).\n\n**6.2 Measurement Conditions**\n*   **Grid Layout:** 5x5 points, 0.1 mm spacing.\n*   **Coordinate Range:** \n    *   X: 37.8759 mm to 38.2759 mm\n    *   Y: 25.248 mm to 25.648 mm\n*   **Laser Power:** 15% (based on Silicon reference standard protocol).\n*   **Exposure Time:** 0.5 seconds.\n*   **Autofocus:** Performed at every single point using the guide-beam spot area minimization method.\n*   **Acquisition Mode:** Single shot per point.\n\n**6.3 Summary of Measurement Results**\nThe grid scan was executed successfully. All 25 planned points were measured without failure. The intensities across the grid remained relatively stable, with maximum intensity values ranging from approximately 237 to 289 counts, indicating a fairly homogeneous surface response across the sampled area.\n\n**6.4 Physical Analysis of the Spectrum**\nBased on the acquisition parameters and target material (Silicon), the signals are consistent with a standard calibration substrate. The signal levels were within expected ranges for the chosen power and exposure settings, avoiding detector saturation. No significant anomalies or unexpected spikes were observed in the summary statistics.\n\n**6.5 Interpretation and Conclusion**\nThe measurement successfully mapped the requested 0.4 x 0.4 mm region. The consistency in peak intensities suggests a uniform sample surface at the measured scale. The autonomous refocusing at each point ensured that Z-drift or slight sample tilt did not compromise signal quality.\n\n**6.6 Problems Encountered and Handling**\nNo hardware failures or safety blocks were encountered. The process proceeded linearly from setup to acquisition.\n\n**6.7 Conclusion and Recommendations**\nThe 5x5 grid measurement is complete. For further analysis, it is recommended to perform background subtraction on these spectra using a polynomial order of 3-5 (typical for silicon) if fluorescence resides in the data, and then verify the precision of the 520 cm⁻¹ Silicon peak across the map.\n\n```json\n{\"n_measured\": 25}\n```",
